  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1140 AM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nTODAY THROUGH THURSDAY...A large, slow moving system will bring \nwidespread rain today, with on and off rain showers lasting through \nWednesday, and possibly into Thursday. No significant impacts are \nexpected. The highest rainfall amounts near 3 inches are near \nAlexandria MN, and this will be more of a soaking rain to recharge \nthe dry conditions. The only flooding concerns would be from ponding \nof water where leaves clog up the drains.\n\nThe first wave of this rainfall is driven by a surge of low level \ntheta_e advection ahead of a upper level PV anomaly within a \nlongwave trough. This will bring most locations the majority of the \nrainfall as it lifts northward today. The lack of instability is the \nlimiting factor on severe weather, but a few rumbles of thunder are \npossible. Meanwhile, strong southeast wind gusts of 30 to 40 mph \nshould remain just below wind advisory criteria. \n\nOvernight, the steady rain will lift north, and the winds will \ndecrease as another PV anomaly rounds the base of the longwave \ntrough, leading to cyclogenesis across the Ozarks. This wave will be \nimpacted by the northern stream ridge, and how these two features \ninteract will determine where the rain falls Tuesday through \nThursday. The current thinking is that the longwave trough will lift \nup over the Upper Mississippi River Valley and stall out, with the \ndeformation band forming most likely west of I-35, on Tuesday, and \nrotating across the region on Wednesday. This means the highest \nrainfall totals continue to be across western and northern \nMinnesota, but the rest of us will still see clouds and scattered \nrain showers from Tuesday through Wednesday, and possibly into \nThursday.\n\nFRIDAY THROUGH SUNDAY...A brief area of high pressure will build in \nacross the region from west to east on Thursday, but clouds and \nshowers could be slow to exit the area. Friday should be dry and \ncool with sun, but another clipper system will move in from the \nnorthwest Friday night into Saturday and bring clouds along with a \nchance for rain on Saturday. This will be a small, quick-moving \nsystem that should exit the region by Sunday allowing drier weather \nto move in for the start of next week.",
